WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.980
Welcome to fascinating frontiers, episode 122.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.460
It's July 5th, 2026.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.260
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.100
Astronomers just found giant planets

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.220
lighter than cotton candy, forcing a rethink

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.560
of how worlds assemble.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.380
Two newly characterized worlds match Jupiter in size

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.480
yet contain far less mass, placing them at the extreme end

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.020
of the super puff category.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.180
The planet's orbit stars roughly 300 light years away

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.060
and were identified through combined transit

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.680
and radial velocity data.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.280
Their densities fall below 0.1 grams per cubic centimeter,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.540
lower than any previously measured gas giant.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.000
Researchers note that standard core accretion models

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.740
struggle to build such voluminous envelopes

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.300
before the protoplanetary disk dissipates.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.120
Follow-up atmospheric observations

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.320
with large ground-based telescopes are already scheduled

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.920
to search for haze or extended envelopes

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.600
that could explain the low densities.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.140
According to reports from Fizz.org,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.460
the combination of transit timing and velocity shifts

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.220
pins the radii near one Jupiter radius,

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.960
while masses sit at only a few tens of earth masses.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.280
Next generation spectrographs on extremely large telescopes

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.540
will soon search for the extended hazes or outflows

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.020
that could explain the observed sizes.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.840
If earth's average density were scaled

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.320
to the size of these worlds,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.300
the resulting object would float in a swimming pool

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.280
like a beach ball.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.000
In reality, the planets formed farther from their stars,

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.200
where cooler temperatures allowed them

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.560
to accrete thick hydrogen helium layers

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.380
before the disk gas dispersed.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.540
Once assembled, the envelopes stayed puffy

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.500
because internal heat from formation

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.280
plus possible tidal heating slowed the cooling

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.940
and contraction that normally shrinks gas giants over time.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.480
One surprising number stands out.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.360
The surface gravity on these worlds

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.920
is only about one-tenth that of Jupiter,

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.520
yet they still hold onto atmospheres

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.340
measured in thousands of kilometers.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.440
The open question is whether the low gravity

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.080
plus stellar irradiation will eventually

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.960
drive hydrodynamic escape that strips the envelopes entirely,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.400
or whether an unseen high altitude haze layer

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.820
can shield the gas long enough for the planets

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.500
to survive to the present day.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.420
From the extreme low-density worlds,

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.080
we now turn to another population of planets,

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.800
whose sheer numbers may soon affect our ability

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.440
to study any worlds at all.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.360
A new analysis quantifies how reflected sunlight

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.740
and radio leakage from the projected mega constellations

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.660
will affect both optical and radio observatories.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.480
The study models cumulative sky brightness increases

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.900
across multiple latitude bands and frequency windows.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.020
Ground-based surveys are expected to lose

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.540
the largest fraction of observing time to satellite trails.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.640
Mitigation proposals include darkening satellite surfaces

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.920
and coordinating with operators on orbital planes.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.020
According to Space News, the projected total reaches

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.220
1.7 million satellites,

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.860
while satellite fleets pose challenges

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.300
for future observations,

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.720
one nearby world is already giving astronomers

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.460
fresh reasons for optimism.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.200
Refined measurements of the planet's radius

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.320
and orbital period have lowered its density estimate

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.380
and placed it closer to the runaway greenhouse boundary

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:31.140
than earlier models suggested.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.780
The world orbits a nearby M dwarf star,

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.060
allowing repeated transit observations

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:40.080
that tighten constraints on possible atmospheres.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.140
Updated climate simulations now include

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.640
the possibility of surface liquid water

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.020
under certain cloud and haze scenarios.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.720
Additional radial velocity data are being collected

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.840
to pin down the planet's mass and bulk composition.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.060
Shifting from individual worlds to the hardware

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.080
that gets us there.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.200
A European launcher developer is quietly upgrading its vehicle.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.580
Adding a fourth-permeetheus engine to the first stage

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.580
would raise expendable low-earth orbit capacity

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:13.440
from four to eight tons without raising average cost per launch.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.160
The change keeps the vehicle in the medium-lift class

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.180
while removing reusability hardware

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.660
for maximum performance.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:24.380
Company engineers state the modification requires

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.240
no increase in structural mass or tank volume.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.940
First flight of the four-engine configuration remains

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:32.760
targeted for 2027.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.980
Another payload heading to orbit soon is not a satellite,

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.700
but a semiconductor test bed riding alongside Starlink.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.200
Two semiconductor processing pods developed

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:48.380
by Bessex Space Industries will ride the Starlink 1050 mission

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.400
scheduled for lift-off at 6.50 AM Eastern Daylight time.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.440
The pods will test thin film deposition

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.860
and etching processes in microgravity for several weeks.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:02.340
Data returned after the test will inform designs

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.040
for future in-space manufacturing platforms.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.180
The mission marks the first orbital demonstration

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.640
of this particular semiconductor hardware.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.140
While new hardware heads upward,

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.380
researchers on the ground are preparing for new horizons

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.400
eventual crossing into true interstellar space.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.740
Southwest Research Institute scientists

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.740
are coupling solar wind measurements

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:27.720
with heliosphere models to predict the location

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.340
of the heliopause boundary.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.640
New horizons will cross.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.200
The method uses existing data from earlier spacecraft

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.280
to calibrate the transition region

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.480
where solar wind pressure equals interstellar pressure.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.520
Accurate forecasts will help the mission team

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.220
schedule instrument operations during the crossing.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:51.180
The spacecraft continues to return data from beyond 55AU.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.540
From the edge of the solar system,

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:56.640
we drop back to our own planet's extreme environments

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.660
being used as stand-ins for future crude missions.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.740
A new study compares human performance metrics collected

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.780
at Antarctic research stations

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.160
with astronaut operational protocols

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.060
used on long-duration flights.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.400
Researchers recorded sleep patterns, cognitive test scores

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.100
and habitat habitability data under extreme isolation.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:21.080
Results indicate that certain geological

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.900
and operational stressors translate directly

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.100
to lunar or Martian surface conditions.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.920
The work supports expanded use of Antarctic sites

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:31.680
for future mission simulations.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.280
In the pre-dawn sky this Sunday,

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.920
Saturn's largest moon Titan appears close to the planet,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.220
joined by several smaller moons

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.240
that modest telescopes can resolve.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:45.600
The alignment lets observers follow the satellites

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.960
changing positions across multiple nights

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.540
without specialized gear,

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.080
beyond steady atmospheric conditions.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:55.540
The same morning also places Mars and Uranus

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.680
in a wide conjunction low in the wind,

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.980
with positions shifting noticeably over the coming week.

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:04.160
Observers are encouraged to log those movements

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:06.800
to track the configurations rapid evolution.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.720
The viewing opportunity comes from astronomy magazines

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.500
July 5th Sky Preview.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:17.860
A 21 panel mosaic assembled at Kagakama Remote Observatory

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.040
captures intricate filaments across one section

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.020
of the Vela Supernova Remnant.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:26.040
Roughly 30 hours of narrow band exposures

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:28.520
were processed separately before final alignment

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.100
to emphasize shock fronts

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:33.140
where expanding debris meets interstellar material.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.100
Continued monitoring over the next decade

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.300
could reveal measurable proper motion

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:39.640
in the brightest filaments.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.300
The image highlights the remnants ongoing interaction

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:44.880
with surrounding gas and dust.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:48.740
The work appears in recent R slash space discussions

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:50.860
of deep sky imaging projects.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:54.860
NASA's Curiosity Rover has started detailed imaging

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:58.300
and compositional analysis of polygonal ground patterns

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:00.160
on the floor of gale crater.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.900
Early spectra point to possible repeated cycles

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.480
of wetting and drying recorded in ancient lake sediments.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.020
Scientists are comparing the structures

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:12.500
with terrestrial analogues to narrow down

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:13.860
formation time scales.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:16.880
Additional traverses will map how widespread

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.500
the polygons remain across the crater floor.

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:22.660
The effort is documented in recent updates

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:24.740
shared on R slash space.

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.900
Astronomers may have caught an early galaxy

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.180
in the process of dying.

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:33.300
A massive system observed 1.4 billion years

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:36.500
after the Big Bang displays extended title tails

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:40.020
and stripped material consistent with ram pressure stripping

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:42.200
by the surrounding intra cluster medium.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:45.780
The galaxy offers a rare snapshot of the shift

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:49.600
from active star formation to quiescence at high red shift.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.840
Further spectroscopy is already underway

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.560
to measure remaining gas mass and current star formation rate.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.800
The findings are reported by Fizz.org.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:02.080
The government accountability office

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:03.920
report highlights rising costs

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.480
for next generation missile warning satellites

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.760
along with gaps in digital engineering practices

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:11.760
within the space force portfolio.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.700
Workforce reductions at launch sites

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.560
add schedule risk for national security missions.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:21.300
The review recommends improved cost estimating models

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:24.340
and earlier integration of commercial launch providers.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:28.540
Congress has requested follow-up briefings within 90 days.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.740
Space news covered the assessment release this week.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.840
60 years ago, the uncrewed AS203 flight

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:39.920
tested the Saturn IB's S4B upper stage in orbit

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:43.260
to qualify restart capability for later lunar missions.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.200
The stage executed a series of burns

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.080
that demonstrated propellant settling

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:50.740
and engine ignition under weightless conditions.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.180
Data from the flight directly informed procedures

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:57.340
later used on Apollo 8 and subsequent missions.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.100
No payload flew beyond instrumentation and cameras.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.000
Recent R space retrospectives marked the anniversary

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:06.860
of the autopolo test flight.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:09.680
Engineers transported the Apple satellite

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:12.820
to an open field for antenna characterization tests

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:16.420
that required a non-magnetic environment in 1981.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.800
The bullet cart provided vibration isolation

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:22.780
and avoided interference from vehicle electronics.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:25.100
The test campaign validated

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:27.160
the satellites communications payload

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:29.420
ahead of its 1981 launch.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:33.600
Historical records from Japan, Korea, and the Arab world

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.480
document a bright new star visible in daylight

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.680
for 23 days on July 4, 1054.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:43.400
The event marked the core collapse death of a star

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:46.260
estimated at 9 to 11 solar masses.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.140
Modern observations confirm the remnant

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.560
contains a pulsar whose spin-down age

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.120
matches the 1054 explosion date.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:57.500
Next time, keep an eye on the first data returns

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:59.440
from those semiconductor test pods

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.440
after the Falcon 9 liftoff.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:03.780
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

